tcptarpit - change history

1.4.4 (2026-10-31)
- fix wrong content in /etc/login.conf.d/tcptarpit on fresh installs:
  @sample copies the last preceding file of the PLIST, and the daemon
  configuration sample stood between the login class sample and its
  @sample directive - so fresh installs wrote the daemon config into
  the login class file (no valid tcptarpit record, usermod failed
  with "No such login class"). Each @sample now directly follows its
  source file. Systems with a pre-existing correct class file never
  triggered this (sample never overwrites existing files).

1.4.3 (2026-10-31)
- fix fresh-install failure found on a production machine: @newuser
  referenced the tcptarpit login class, but pkg_add runs @newuser
  before any file is extracted - the class file from @sample does
  not exist yet, so useradd failed ("No such login class"), the
  _tcptarpit user was never created and the run directory and log
  file @execs failed as well. @newuser now creates the user without
  a class; the existing usermod @exec assigns the class after
  cap_mkdb has registered it. Test systems with a pre-existing user
  never triggered this.

1.4.2 (2026-10-31)
- production hardening from the final pre-deployment audit, three
  edge-case defects fixed:
  * busy loop: a connection that reached the dribble cap kept a stale
    stutter deadline and pinned the select timeout at zero (100 % CPU
    with large max_age values); capped connections no longer
    participate in the deadline computation
  * blocking write: the stutter byte was written to blocking sockets,
    so a full send buffer (peer never reads, small tcp sendspace)
    could freeze the whole event loop; client sockets are non-blocking
    now and EAGAIN suspends the dribble for that connection, read
    side handles spurious EAGAIN wakeups
  * accept spin: at the fd limit with connections still arriving, the
    readable listening socket caused an unlogged full-CPU loop;
    accept failures are now logged once and paced at 10 retries/s
    (EMFILE/ENFILE only - ECONNABORTED from handshake races under
    churn is expected and skipped silently; found on the test system
    where pacing it throttled accept until client connects timed out)

1.4.1 (2026-10-31)
- documentation overhaul: README brought up to the state of 1.4.0
  (stutter and IPv6 defaults, file logging with newsyslog rotation,
  login class, pkg-readme, pledge rpath/unveil cr findings, both
  tested releases 7.9 and 8.0). No daemon code changes.

1.4.0 (2026-10-31)
- file logging is now the default: log_file defaults to
  /var/log/tcptarpit.log and pid_file to
  /var/run/tcptarpit/tcptarpit.pid, keeping /var/log/daemon free of
  tarpit messages. The package creates the log file (owned by
  _tcptarpit, 0640) and the run directory, and appends an idempotent
  newsyslog(8) rotation entry to /etc/newsyslog.conf (newsyslog has
  no include directory, so a marked block is appended and stays
  editable). SIGHUP reopens the log after rotation, signaled via the
  pid file. Empty log_file restores the previous stdout/syslog
  behaviour.
- three shutdown/rotation defects found by the OpenBSD 8.0 tests and
  fixed: a failed log reopen lost all further messages (now falls
  back to stdout and retries on every SIGHUP); every SIGTERM/SIGINT
  ended in a pledge abort because perl's unlink() lstat()s the pid
  file (rpath is now pledged when a pid file is configured); and the
  pid file survived the shutdown because the directory unveil alone
  does not permit the removal (the file itself is unveiled with "cr"
  now).

1.3.0 (2026-10-31)
- stutter mode like spamd(8): by default one filler byte is dribbled
  back per connection per second, so trapped clients keep waiting for
  a real response; stutter=0 restores pure silence. The dribble is
  capped per connection (32 KiB) so the send buffer can never fill
  and block the daemon; write errors close the connection with the
  usual abort logging.
- IPv6 support: the socket layer moved from IO::Socket::INET to
  IO::Socket::IP (part of the OpenBSD base perl). IPv4 and IPv6 are
  both enabled by default (127.0.0.1 and ::1) and each family is
  switched off individually by leaving listen_ip or listen_ip6 empty;
  at least one address must remain. Peer addresses are logged in
  their family's notation. pf needs separate inet6 rules with
  rdr-to ::1 (documented with examples). The load test gained a
  v6_probe phase and runs over IO::Socket::IP as well.

1.2.1 (2026-10-31)
- pkg-readme: the package installs the post-installation guide as
  /usr/local/share/doc/pkg-readmes/tcptarpit (the OpenBSD convention
  for package readmes) and displays it after installation via the
  @display PLIST directive: enable/start, pf integration, config and
  address management at a glance.

1.2.0 (2026-10-31)
- dedicated login class for the daemon: the package installs
  /etc/login.conf.d/tcptarpit (@sample, cap_mkdb runs automatically)
  with openfiles-cur=1024/openfiles-max=4096, and @newuser assigns the
  class directly (the loginclass field); upgrades set it via
  usermod. Without the class the daemon stops accepting at the
  default 128 fd soft limit (found and verified with the new load
  test). The class file is self-contained because cap_mkdb builds it
  standalone - no tc= interpolation from login.conf.
- tests/: load and resource test suite (tests/tarpit-loadtest.pl)
  with baseline probe, 300-connection idle flood, 100 MiB data flood,
  1500-cycle FIN/RST churn, slowloris, 450-connection fd pressure and
  recovery verification; measures daemon CPU, RSS and fd usage per
  phase. Verified on OpenBSD 8.0: all phases pass with the login
  class active (450 held connections, 457 fds, CPU +1 s for 100 MiB,
  full recovery to baseline).

1.1 (2026-10-31)
- log how long a bot endured the tarpit: the "gave up" and "aborted"
  messages now include a readable duration (e.g. "1 min 20 s"); the
  hold-time kick message uses the same format
- rc.d(8) script: run the interpreter as daemon and the script as
  daemon_flags. OpenBSD 7.9's rc.subr rebuilds pexp from daemon +
  daemon_flags (custom pexp values are overwritten), so check, stop
  and restart now actually match the running process
- port: DISTFILES= (empty) is the correct knob for the distfile-less
  build; uid/gid 960 must be registered in
  /usr/ports/infrastructure/db/user.list for @newuser/@newgroup
- port: install this file as share/doc/tcptarpit/CHANGES
- ISC licence added (LICENCE); the port declares it with
  PERMIT_PACKAGE and the customary "# ISC" comment
- README: the pf.conf example now includes a stateful
  `pass out ... keep state` rule - building a complete ruleset from
  the fragment without it silently breaks the system's own outgoing
  traffic (replies match the inbound block; found with `pfctl -d`
  during the OpenBSD 7.9 tests)

1.0 (2026-10-31)
- initial release: generic layer-4 TCP tarpit behind pf rdr-to,
  protocol agnostic, never sends a single byte back
- fix fd leak in the cleanup pass: each() yields stringified hash
  keys, so close() and IO::Select::remove() on them silently failed;
  the real handle is now stored in the connection record and kicked
  connections really receive a FIN
- drain sockets in 64 KiB chunks instead of 1 byte per readable event
  (1 MB flood cost ~11 s CPU before, ~0 s after)
- distinguish client FIN, client RST and daemon-side hold-time
  timeout in the log; connections reset before accept() completes are
  dropped without registration
- /etc/tcptarpit.conf with sane built-in defaults and fail-fast
  validation: listen_ip, listen_port, cleanup_interval, max_age,
  log_file, pid_file, user, group
- optional file logging with SIGHUP reopen for newsyslog(8) rotation
  and a pid file for signaling
- privilege drop via user/group keys; rc script runs the daemon as
  the unprivileged _tcptarpit user created automatically by pkg_add
- pledge(2) and unveil(2) self-restriction on OpenBSD before the
  event loop starts
- OpenBSD port skeleton (@newuser/@newgroup, @rcscript, @sample)
  building an installable tcptarpit-<version>.tgz
